description:
FILED s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642 = MAIN-READY-111, v2-16300) by the Rosetta census (docs/rosetta-plan.md step 1; #2104).

THE SHAPE (neutral reproducers, run on the tree, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL):

    package Num;
    use overload '/' => sub { wantarray ? ("L1", "L2") : "scalar" };
    sub new { bless {}, shift }
    package main;
    my $x = Num->new;
    my @r = ($x / 2);
    print scalar(@r), "\n";              # perl 1        PCL 2

    use Math::BigInt;
    my $y = Math::BigInt->new(7);
    my @q = ($y / 2);           print scalar(@q), " [@q]\n";   # perl 1 [3]   PCL 2 [3 1]
    my @s = (scalar($y / 2));   print scalar(@s), " [@s]\n";   # perl 1 [3]   PCL 1 [2]   (scalar() counts the pair)
    sub half { my $v = shift; return -1 if $v < 0; $v / 2 }
    my @t = half($y);           print scalar(@t), " [@t]\n";   # perl 1 [3]   PCL 2 [3 1]

perlovl: the implementation of an operator is called "in scalar context" -- an operator yields one value.  PCL calls the handler with the surrounding `*wantarray*`, so a handler that answers by context (Math::BigInt/BigFloat/BigRat `bdiv` returns (quo, rem) in list context, by its documented API) leaks a list.  `print $x / 2` happens to be right (scalar there), so the visible shapes are a list assignment, a sub's tail expression in a list-context call, a `map` body, a constructor's argument list.

WHERE: the overload dispatch of a binary operator (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp `p-find-overload` and its callers, docs/method-lookup-eval-s534.md) calls the handler without binding `*wantarray*` to scalar (nil); the fix is one binding at the dispatch.  `scalar(...)` around the operator does NOT help (the handler already ran in list context; `scalar` then counts) -- only an assignment to a scalar does, which is the cheap discriminating measurement:

CHEAP DISCRIMINATING MEASUREMENT (taken): in a scratch copy of the program (with #2560 patched by building each fraction from two Math::BigInt objects), rewriting the recursive sub's tail `(A)/(B)` into `my $r = (A)/(B); $r` makes all of its output identical to perl.

THE PROGRAM: 0195-Check-Machin-like-formulas (Math::BigRat): the arctangent-addition sub ends in a `/` of two rationals and is called in list context (a two-element `my (...) = (f(...), f(...))`), so each call returns quotient AND remainder; it also needs #2560 (a "N/D" string constructor).

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 0ab08642) === Rosetta 0567-Getting-the-number-of-decimal-places holds this cause too (`use bignum`; a division of two literals in a `for` list gives quotient AND remainder, two loop items).  The handler sees the DYNAMIC `*wantarray*`, not the operator's own context: even a scalar initializer inside a list-context `do` block runs it as a list --
    use bignum;
    print "[$_]\n" for 9, do { my $q = 1/3; $q };     # perl [9] [0.333...3]      PCL [9] [0] [1]   ($q holds the (quotient, remainder) pair)
    my @l = (1/3); print scalar(@l), "\n";            # perl 1                    PCL 2
  (emitted: `(p-list-ctx (funcall (lambda () ... (p-let (($q :scalar (p-/ ...))) ...))))` -- the `:scalar` init does not rebind *wantarray*, so the fix belongs at the overload call, not at each syntactic site).  Hoisting the division to a file-level `my` makes 0567 identical to perl -- its only cause.
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 0195, 0567)

=== s543p (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27, CODE 496df8b1 = MAIN-READY-113) === Rosetta 1473-Tuppers-self-referential-formula holds this cause too: a chain of Math::BigInt `/` inside a `map` block (list context) -- every `/` returns quotient AND remainder.  Even a SCALAR ASSIGNMENT inside the map body does not shield the handler (the dynamic `*wantarray*` is still true), which is the strongest form of the finding:
    use Math::BigInt;
    my $y = Math::BigInt->new("1000");
    my @r = map { $y / 7 } 1;                      print scalar(@r), " [@r]\n";   # perl 1 [142]   PCL 2 [142 6]
    my @s = map { my $q; $q = $y / 7; $q } 1;      print scalar(@s), " [@s]\n";   # perl 1 [142]   PCL 2 [142 6]
  Moving the arithmetic into a helper sub called in scalar context (`my $b = bit(...)`) makes 1473 identical to perl -- its only cause.
LABEL: silent (ordinary: Rosetta 0195, 0567, 1473)
